## What a `type: frontmatter` instance is

A first-class representation of LONG-TAIL frontmatter — keys present in a memorial's YAML frontmatter that DON'T have a corresponding `:mm.memory/*` slot declaration.  Rather than dropping unknown keys at parse time, the codec preserves them via a `:mm/Frontmatter` entity referenced from the memorial's `:mm.memory/frontmatter` slot.  The extra keys are serialized as EDN-encoded structured data in `:mm.frontmatter/extra`.

## When `:mm/Frontmatter` materializes

The codec materializes a `:mm/Frontmatter` entity ONLY when a memorial's frontmatter has keys that aren't declared on its class.  Memorials with fully-declared frontmatter have no `:mm.memory/frontmatter` ref (the slot is nil).

This is the "extras" capture pattern — preserves data the substrate doesn't formally typify while still being round-trip-stable.

## When to promote vs keep as extras

Extras are FINE for genuinely one-off or transient keys.  Promote to a first-class `:mm.<class>/*` slot when:

- The key recurs across multiple memorials (≥3 occurrences)
- The key has stable substrate-level semantics (not just informal)
- Queries against the key would benefit from indexed storage

Per the predicate-vocabulary-gap pattern, schema extension supersedes extras-capture for recurring patterns.

